.... OpenID4Java Library - INSTALL

========================================================================

INSTALLATION AND USAGE INSTRUCTIONS

Introduction:

This document describes how to build and use the OpenID4Java library.
For general information about the package please see the README.md file.


Requirements:

  - JDK 17 or newer. The build is tested on JDK 17, 21 and 25.
  - Maven 3.9 or newer.


Building:

  Run the full build and test suite:

        mvn clean verify

  This produces:

        target/openid4java-<version>.jar
        target/openid4java-<version>-sources.jar
        target/openid4java-<version>-javadoc.jar

  To install the artifacts into the local Maven repository:

        mvn install


Using the library:

  Add the dependency to your project:

        <dependency>
          <groupId>com.amarulasolutions</groupId>
          <artifactId>openid4java</artifactId>
          <version>2.0.0</version>
        </dependency>

  The Amarula Solutions fork is published to GitHub Packages. Add the
  repository to your project:

        <repositories>
          <repository>
            <id>github</id>
            <url>https://maven.pkg.github.com/amarula/openid4java</url>
          </repository>
        </repositories>

  GitHub Packages requires authentication. Add a server entry to
  ~/.m2/settings.xml with your GitHub username and a personal access
  token that has the read:packages scope:

        <servers>
          <server>
            <id>github</id>
            <username>YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME</username>
            <password>YOUR_PAT</password>
          </server>
        </servers>

  The core dependencies are resolved transitively by Maven.

  For Jenkins plugins, use the shaded artifact:

        <dependency>
          <groupId>com.amarulasolutions</groupId>
          <artifactId>openid4java-shaded</artifactId>
          <version>2.0.0</version>
        </dependency>

  Its third-party packages are relocated under org.openid4java.shaded.*;
  commons-codec is an external dependency and slf4j-api is provided by
  the runtime (Jenkins provides it).

  The Spring JDBC and Ehcache integrations are optional. Add spring-jdbc
  or ehcache explicitly when using JdbcConsumerAssociationStore,
  JdbcServerAssociationStore, JdbcNonceVerifier or EhcacheNonceVerifier.

  Logging uses Apache Commons Logging.


Usage examples:

  See README.md and the Javadoc overview for the Relying Party and
  OpenID Provider usage patterns.

  A complete Relying Party implementation is in:
        test/src/org/openid4java/consumer/SampleConsumer.java

  A complete Provider implementation is in:
        test/src/org/openid4java/server/SampleServer.java


Samples:

  See samples/README for the available sample projects.


Contacts:

  For support and general discussion, subscribe to the Google Group:

        http://groups.google.com/group/openid4java


Thanks for running OpenID!


